"""Chapter 9: Conclusion and Future Enhancements"""


def build(r):
    r.chapter("Conclusion and Future Enhancements")
    r.h1("9.1", "Conclusion")
    r.p("The AI-Powered Intelligent Recruitment System shows that resume screening can be fast, explainable and fair at the same time. It replaces the keyword score of the first phase by a decision process that treats every skill as a claim that needs evidence. A skill that is demonstrated in experience or project text counts fully, a skill that is only listed counts half, and a related skill counts half through an explicit, auditable rule. These credits feed a seven-factor hybrid ranker and a four-agent council, so that the final verdict can be traced to the features, rules and disagreements that produced it.")
    r.p("The system was implemented as a Python core library, a FastAPI service with a relational data model, a Streamlit workspace and a React web interface. The web interface and the API are deployed as a multi-user service with server-side accounts (salted PBKDF2 hashes, expiring sessions and verified Google sign-in) and a managed MongoDB Atlas database. The core includes a resume parser with an OCR fallback, a 79-skill ontology, a hybrid ranker, a resume quality analyser, a blind-screening filter, a council with knock-out gates, red flags, confidence and human-review triggers, a GitHub authenticity inspector, a hybrid talent search, an interview-question generator and a retrieval-grounded recruiter chatbot. A suite of 78 automated tests covers these components, the account service and the logic of the web interface, and all of them pass.")
    r.p("The evaluation on synthetic resume pools gave the following results. A complete screening takes about 30 milliseconds per resume in the offline configuration. The keyword-stuffed test resume that a keyword baseline scores at 47.1 percent, the same as a genuine four-year engineer, is ranked tenth of twelve and rejected, while the strong test candidate is ranked first with 88.1 percent. The scores and the verdicts did not change in 96 name-substitution runs, and the rankings were stable under random changes of the weights by 20 percent (mean Kendall rank correlation 0.969). Explicit timeline gates intercepted resumes with inverted dates and concurrent full-time employment that similarity scoring could not see.")
    r.p("The evaluation also showed the limits of the work. All data are synthetic and were written with knowledge of the rules, so the results show consistency and mechanism and not accuracy on real hiring outcomes. On a 100-resume benchmark the council was conservative at the top (no strong-hire verdict) and permissive at the bottom (11 of 35 reject profiles rejected). The weights and thresholds are chosen by the developers, blind screening removes only direct identifiers, no usability study has been carried out, and the deployed service runs on free cloud tiers that have not been load-tested. The system should therefore be used as a decision-support tool with a human in the loop, and not as an autonomous decision-maker. Overall, the project shows that explainable, evidence-based and bias-aware screening is practical, transparent and fast enough for everyday use.")

    r.h1("9.2", "Future Enhancement")
    r.p("The following enhancements can improve the system.", keep=True)
    r.bullets([
        "**Evaluation on real data:** a labelled evaluation on uncurated applicant pools, with recruiter agreement and ranking metrics such as nDCG as ground truth, is the decisive test of the system. Held-out adversarial resumes written independently of the gate rules should be added.",
        "**Learned and calibrated weights:** the weights, the thresholds and the verdict boundaries can be learned and calibrated from recruiter feedback, so that the council issues strong-hire verdicts where recruiters would.",
        "**Group-fairness measurement:** metrics such as the adverse-impact ratio and FA*IR-style ranking constraints [@fair] should be measured on demographically labelled data, and bias tests should be extended to the dense embeddings and to language-model narratives [@wilson,@armstrong].",
        "**Richer evidence:** live validation of the GitHub inspector on real candidate profiles, additional evidence sources such as LinkedIn and portfolios, and validation of certificates with their issuers.",
        "**Larger ontology and multilingual resumes:** extension of the skill ontology beyond 79 skills, possibly by mapping to a public taxonomy, and support for resumes in more languages.",
        "**Retrieval evaluation:** evaluation of the dense and hybrid search with the sentence-embedding model on larger pools with paraphrased queries, and the use of a cross-encoder for fine re-ranking.",
        "**API tests, stored history and e-mail:** automated tests for the relational REST endpoints (the account and candidate endpoints are already tested), persistence of screening runs and recruiter decisions for each account in the managed database (the accounts, the sessions and the candidate pools are already stored in MongoDB Atlas), and delivery of interview invitations by e-mail through the API, which the web interface is already prepared to use through configuration.",
        "**Usability study and production deployment:** a usability study with practising recruiters, a load test of the deployed service, and the step from the present free-tier deployment to a production platform with role-based access, candidate notice and consent, data-retention limits and regular fairness audits.",
    ])
